<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  billet compute host, for an Apple Silicon Mac running the tart backend.

  NOT LOADED BY ANY PACKAGE.

    cp sh.billet.node.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/sh.billet.node.plist

  A LAUNCH AGENT, NOT A LAUNCH DAEMON, and that is the whole difference between
  this file and billet-node.service. Three facts force it:

    1. Since macOS 15, Virtualization.framework requires an UNLOCKED
       login.keychain to run a VM — undocumented, and tart's own FAQ is where it
       is written down. A headless SSH session leaves that keychain locked, and
       the guest then fails with "SecKeyCreateRandomKey_ios failed", "Failed to
       generate keypair", or "Interaction is not allowed with the Security
       Server" — none of which mention a keychain. A daemon has no login session
       and so has no unlocked keychain.
    2. tart's VM store is per-user (TART_HOME, defaulting to ~/.tart). A daemon
       running as root would look at root's store and find none of the images
       the operator pulled.
    3. The Linux node unit runs as ROOT because Docker's socket and the
       Firecracker jailer need it. Neither applies here: tart is a CLI the
       operator's own account drives, and running this as root would buy nothing
       while breaking (1) and (2).

  So a Mac node is a logged-in user's agent. On a dedicated host that means
  enabling automatic login, which is a real security decision — the disk is
  unlocked and the session is live at every boot — and it is the price of
  running macOS guests without a person present. See docs/reference/reference-hardware.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>sh.billet.node</string>

    <!-- The config flag is EXPLICIT, for the reason the systemd units are explicit:
         billet's default config path is per-user and never reads the working
         directory. A system path also keeps this file free of a username, which
         launchd would not expand anyway — it performs no variable substitution
         in ProgramArguments or in the log paths below. -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/local/bin/billet</string>
        <string>node</string>
        <string>--config</string>
        <string>/usr/local/etc/billet/billet.yaml</string>
    </array>

    <!-- A LAUNCH AGENT DOES NOT INHERIT YOUR SHELL'S PATH, and without this the
         node starts, registers, and can do nothing: measured on a real Mac, the
         first thing it says is

           tart: list VMs: exec: "tart": executable file not found in $PATH

         and it then refuses to take work, correctly, because it cannot tell
         what is already running here. launchd's own default is
         /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, which contains neither Homebrew prefix.

         SOFTNET IS RESOLVED THE SAME WAY, by billet's preflight and by tart
         itself when it supervises an isolated guest — so a PATH missing the
         Homebrew prefix breaks untrusted isolation as well as inventory.

         Both prefixes: /opt/homebrew is Apple Silicon Homebrew, /usr/local is
         where a manually installed billet and an Intel Homebrew put things. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Restart on failure only, which is Restart=on-failure. A node that exits
         0 has finished draining and must stay stopped; restarting it would take
         new work the operator asked it to stop taking. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <!-- THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS FILE. launchd sends SIGTERM, waits
         ExitTimeOut, then SIGKILLs — and the default is FIVE SECONDS, measured
         on macOS 26 by asking `launchctl print` about an agent that sets none.
         (Apple's launchd.plist(5) says twenty; the machine says five, and the
         machine is what runs the node.) billet's node answers SIGTERM by
         DRAINING: it stops taking work and waits for the jobs already running
         here, which is bounded by drain_timeout and can be hours. A SIGKILL
         through the middle of that leaves guests running with their leases
         renewed by nobody, which is exactly what TimeoutStopSec=88200 prevents
         on Linux.

         Sized the same way: the maximum configurable drain (24h) plus teardown
         plus margin. Lower drain_timeout first if this is too long to wait at
         shutdown; never this alone.

         NOT ZERO. launchd reads zero as infinity, and the man page's own
         warning is that it can stall system shutdown forever. -->
    <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
    <integer>88200</integer>

    <!-- Interactive, so launchd does not apply the background I/O and CPU
         throttling it gives Standard and Background jobs. This process
         supervises virtual machines running somebody's CI. -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Interactive</string>

    <!-- RestartSec=5s. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>5</integer>

    <!-- launchd does not expand ~ or $HOME here either, so these are absolute
         and the directory has to exist and be writable by this user:

           sudo mkdir -p /usr/local/var/log/billet
           sudo chown "$(id -u):$(id -g)" /usr/local/var/log/billet -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/usr/local/var/log/billet/node.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/usr/local/var/log/billet/node.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
